background:
On August 17, 2026, it was reported that [US President Trump spoke to Fox News](https://www.foxnews.com/world/trump-confirms-irgc-backchannel-calls-irans-white-flag-surrender) about the ongoing challenges negotiating with Iran in the ongoing war surrounding the Strait of Hormuz.

> If Oman gets in the way, we’ll bomb the s--- out of them.

This is not the first time that the US President has threatened the Gulf nation. Back in May 2026, it was reported that [he warned Oman](https://edition.cnn.com/2026/05/27/world/live-news/iran-war-us-news#cmpoc4ge900003b6u9s3zjw35) not to interfere in the US negotiations with Iran.&#x20;

> Oman will behave just like everybody else or we’ll have to blow ‘em up.

Despite [long-standing economic and military US-Oman ties](https://www.aspistrategist.org.au/how-to-lose-friends-and-alienate-allies-trumps-approach-to-oman/), Oman's intermediary role in the US-Israel war with Iran has strained recent relations.&#x20;

comments.text:
Likelihood : 0.1%&#x20;

Oman serves as a the gateway between all negotiations. Remember 2021 Doha Agreement. It is not firmly embedded as a mediator.&#x20;

Also its mediation predates this conflict.&#x20;
So a firm no&#x20;

**Likelihood: 13%**

**Reasons:**&#x20;

1. Threats are **coercive rhetoric** not operational intent yet.&#x20;
2. Military action require US to **attack a militarily weak ally that provides critical military access** (Duqm port)
3. **Strategic costs** i.e. Gulf alliance collapse, loss of bases, Iranian propaganda victory & domestic political backlash **outweigh any benefit** (strait remains closed either way).
4. 13% accounts for (*2  x aggresive people who control US military i.e. Trump & Pete Hegseth)* & risk of miscalculation if Oman finalizes its Iran deal which Trump interprets as direct challenge and/or if any Hormuz incident involving Omani waters escalates beyond control
5. **The 40 Trillion dollar debt is also a factor in following way**; Attack/threat Oman to force it to pressure Iran into reopening the SoH, which would crash oil prices, may allow the Federal Reserve to slash interest rates which in turn **slashes the US government's annual interest payments on its \$40 trillion debt by hundreds of billions of dollars**&#x20;

Oman is not the main player or say an albatross around the US's neck, but Trump is mercurial and acts on his whims.&#x20;


Let's delve into some key points:

* US's stockpile of weapons is getting low, CSIS posted an article mentioning the count of remaining stockpiles, while I know that it's not critically low but that's something to consider as the US is waging war on Iran. Attacking Oman will not result in anything even if they (Oman) surrender controlling strait of Hormuz (Oman's territorial waterway) the two-way traffic idea is still impractical as Iran can attack ships going through Oman's waterways.

* Oman's Foreign minister, Badr Al Busaidi recently wrote an article in Financial Times. He basically argues that modern diplomacy is failing to deliver a lasting resolution to global conflicts. He further said, the recent decline in adherence to international law, and increased use of assassination with abduction as a tool of policy to achieve goals with reckless attacks of civilians and open contempt for institutes like UN, International Court of Justice and International Criminal Court would set the ground for an era where global powers could act with impunity. He pointed out that world leaders are attempting to conduct a diplomacy without involving experienced diplomats, and without these diplomats the inexperienced ones tend to coercive tactics. They are forced to resort either to the traditional 19-century "gunboat diplomacy" or more modern and refined approach of "economic bullying" with the aim to force entire population into submission.&#x20;

  Oman's Foreign Minister has outlined three objective;
  1 - protecting Omani sovereignty
  2 - protecting international interests
  3 - Affirming international law.

  Oman has been playing a neutral role since the start and is more inclined toward constructive neutrality. I think it would really be a fool's errand to attack Oman as they are not holding the cards, whereas Iran is the one who is incharge and calling the shots.



US is fully aware that its gunboat diplomacy is clearly failing and bombing the aggressor often creates a rally-round-the-flag effect, which obviously further complicates the situation. Trump is mercurial, but he often TACOs.
I think Einstein's quote "Insanity is doing the same thing over and over again and expecting different results" describes this dilemma perfectly. Bombing and coercing will not result in the desired effect or a surrender, and Trump has been sufficiently informed by his intelligence agencies about this.&#x20;

**Diplomacy is the only way going forward, I hope "cooler heads prevail"**&#x20;

Oman has long functioned as in intermediary in many conflicts in the middle east (see [https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366050613\_Omani\_Cultural\_Power\_and\_Diplomacy\_Uniqueness](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366050613_Omani_Cultural_Power_and_Diplomacy_Uniqueness) or [https://www.researchgate.net/publication/303421853\_Oman\_culture\_and\_diplomacy](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/303421853_Oman_culture_and_diplomacy) for example)

This means that

&#x20;1\) it has a experienced diplomatic corps, which makes wars of mistake less likely, since the diplomats have a lot of experience working with the US and understand their thinking.&#x20;

And&#x20;

2\) Oman is useful to the US as a diplomatic mediator (including in conflicts such as the Iran war, yemen civil war, etc.). Attacking Oman would destroy this useful relationship, which means that it would have a substantial cost (negative impact) to the US too, even though they are militarily dominant.&#x20;

Oman has about 40,000 personnel, a few small corvettes, and less fighter jets than a US aircraft carrier (of outdated types). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultan's\_Armed\_Forces

This means that they would lose any conflict with the US or at least suffer huge losses and infrastructure damage. Compared to Iran they have far less experience with active wars, and they lack a drone or ballistic missile program so they likely wouldn't be able to cause much damage in return.&#x20;

This means that there is a strong incentive for Oman to do whatever is necessary to prevent a war with the US. I therefore think that a consciously chosen war is extremely unlikely, though a war by mistake (e.g. diplomatic missteps, wrong estimates about US red lines) may still be possible
